plot.pdf}\n  \\caption{A caption with words that can be rewritten.}\n  \\label{fig:plot}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{tabular}{l|r}\n  Name & Value \\\\ \\hline\n  alpha & 1 \\\\\n\\end{tabular}\n\n$$ \\int_0^\\infty e^{-x}\\,dx = 1 $$\n\nSpecial characters: ~ & ^ _ { } and a nested \\textbf{\\emph{\\textsc{group}}}.\n\n\\printbibliography\n\\end{document}\n"],"out":[]},{"args":["<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <title>Edge cases</title>\n  <style>body { font-family: serif; } p > em { color: red; }</style>\n  <script>const words = \"do not touch\"; if (a < b) { run(); }</script>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <!-- a comment -->\n  <h1 class=\"title\">A heading worth rewriting</h1>\n  <p>This paragraph has <strong>bold</strong> and <a href=\"https://example.com/?q=1&amp;r=2\">a link</a> in it.\n  Entities like &nbsp;, &eacute; and &#169; stay as they are.</p>\n  <pre>preformatted   text\n  is protected</pre>\n  <p>Inline <code>code()</code> is protected too.</p>\n</body>\n</html>\n"],"out":[]},{"args":["# Why small models matter\n\nIn recent years, a large number of companies have started to rely on huge language models. However, these models are expensive to run, and they send your data to someone else's servers.\n\nSmall models that run on your own computer are a good alternative. They are fast, private, and they work without an internet connection. For example, a model with 100 million parameters can easily run on a laptop.\n\n## Getting started\n\nInstall the package with `pip install something` and read the [documentation](https://example.com/docs) in order to understand the options.\n\n```python\n# code blocks are never changed\nmodel = load(\"small-model\")\n```\n\nIt is important to note that small models are not perfect. They make mistakes, so you should always check the results carefully.\n"],"out":[[27,42,"In recent years"],[44,61,"a large number of"],[88,95,"rely on"],[351,362,"For example"],[553,564,"in order to"],[665,693,"It is important to note that"]]},{"args":["\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\title{Efficient Training of Language Models}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nIn recent years, deep learning has had an impact on a wide range of fields, as shown in \\cite{lecun2015}. However, training large models requires a significant amount of computational resources. In order to address this problem, we propose a simple method that reduces the cost of training by $50\\%$ while maintaining accuracy.\n\nOur approach plays an important role in making research more accessible. The results clearly demonstrate that the method works on all benchmarks (see Table~\\ref{tab:results}).\n\n\\begin{equation}\n  L(\\theta) = -\\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\log p_\\theta(x_i)\n\\end{equation}\n\n% TODO: add related work\n\\section{Method}\nWe carefully analyse the loss $L(\\theta)$ and \\textbf{significantly} reduce the number of gradient steps. It is important to note that this does not require any additional data.\n\n\\end{document}\n"],"out":[[143,158,"In recent years"],[178,194,"had an impact on"],[195,210,"a wide range of"],[219,230,"as shown in"],[289,312,"a significant amount of"],[338,349,"In order to"],[372,382,"we propose"],[485,511,"plays an important role in"],[879,907,"It is important to note that"]]},{"args":["#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"A module docstring is a string, not a comment.\"\"\"\nimport os  # this comment is prose that can be rewritten\n\n\ndef area(r):\n    # Compute the area of a circle with the given radius.\n    return 3.14159 * r * r  # a trailing comment\n\n\nURL = \"https://example.com/#not-a-comment\"\n"],"out":[]},{"args":["/* A block comment that spans\n   two lines of prose. */\n#include <stdio.h>\n\n// A line comment explaining the function below.\nint main(void) {\n    const char *s = \"// not a comment\";\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", s); /* trailing block */\n    return 0;\n}\n"],"out":[]},{"args":["-- Select every user who signed up this year.\nSELECT name, email